
<p>That was what made Alaric suspicious.</p>
<p>He found Gaston near the musicians.</p>
<p>“Who was the man in the dark green coat?”</p>
<p>Gaston followed his glance.</p>
<p>“Sir Aubert. Bernier’s household.”</p>
<p>“And the one with the red collar?”</p>
<p>“Steward to a minor lord from the northern marches. Why?”</p>
<p>“They are all leaving separately.”</p>
<p>Gaston frowned.</p>
<p>“That may mean nothing.”</p>
<p>“Yes.”</p>
<p>Alaric started walking.</p>
<p>“That is why I am going to find out.”</p>
<p>The corridors beyond the great hall were quieter.</p>
<p>Servants moved between kitchens and storerooms. Guards stood at stairways. Alaric passed through the gallery overlooking the kitchens and reached a narrow passage leading toward an older part of Côte de Lys.</p>
<p>There he heard voices.</p>
<p>Not enough to understand words.</p>
<p>Enough to hear urgency.</p>
<p>He moved closer.</p>
<p>A door stood slightly open.</p>
<p>Inside, Bernier spoke to three men.</p>
<p>“…before dawn. Once Raymond is outside Bellacourt, Charles has a choice.”</p>
<p>Another voice answered.</p>
<p>“And if he refuses?”</p>
<p>“Then his heir remains unavailable when Lothar moves.”</p>
<p>Alaric froze.</p>
<p>Lothar.</p>
<p>The name meant nothing to him yet.</p>
<p>The tone did.</p>
<p>A board creaked beneath his boot.</p>
<p>Silence.</p>
<p>Alaric stepped back just as the door opened.</p>
<p>Sir Aubert saw him.</p>
<p>For half a heartbeat neither moved.</p>
<p>Then Aubert drew a dagger.</p>
<p>Alaric caught his wrist, drove him into the wall and shouted for the guard.</p>
<p>The corridor exploded into motion.</p>
<p>Bernier did not try to fight.</p>
<p>He ran.</p>
<p>One conspirator overturned a lamp. Another slammed a side door behind him. Guards came from the stairwell while servants scattered.</p>
<p>Alaric chased Bernier through two chambers and into the inner court.</p>
<p>By the time he reached it, horses were already pounding through the lower gate.</p>
<p>Bernier was not among them.</p>
<p>Charles arrived moments later with Raymond, Gaston and several royal guards.</p>
<p>“What happened?”</p>
<p>Alaric pointed toward the gate.</p>
<p>“Bernier is moving against you. He spoke of Raymond. And someone named Lothar.”</p>
<p>Charles’s face hardened.</p>
<p>Raymond looked more angry than afraid.</p>
<p>“Where is Bernier?”</p>
<p>“Gone or hiding.”</p>
<p>A captain ran into the court.</p>
<p>“Your Majesty. There has been fighting at the east service gate. Three men escaped.”</p>
<p>“Close the city,” Charles ordered.</p>
<p>The captain hesitated.</p>
<p>“All gates? During the tournament?”</p>
<p>“All gates.”</p>
<p>The order spread through Côte de Lys.</p>
<p>Bells began ringing again.</p>
<p>Not the bells of sickness this time.</p>
<p>Alarm bells.</p>
<p>Within minutes, soldiers were moving toward the walls.</p>
<p>The feast broke apart.</p>
<p>Envoys demanded explanations. Nobles gathered their retainers. Priests tried to calm servants and pages. Somewhere in the hall, the musicians stopped playing halfway through a song.</p>
<p>Alaric found Raymond fastening on a sword belt.</p>
<p>“Stay inside the citadel,” Alaric said.</p>
<p>Raymond looked offended.</p>
<p>“I am not a vase.”</p>
<p>“No. You are exactly what Bernier intends to take.”</p>
<p>“Which is why hiding makes me useful to him.”</p>
<p>“Being alive and unkidnapped also makes you useful to Charles.”</p>
<p>Raymond opened his mouth.</p>
<p>Then the outer horn sounded.</p>
<p>A royal guard burst through the archway.</p>
<p>“Lord Raymond. Your men at the eastern lodging request you immediately. One of your household has been wounded.”</p>
<p>Raymond was already moving.</p>
<p>Alaric caught his arm.</p>
<p>“Wait.”</p>
<p>The messenger looked confused.</p>
<p>“Sir?”</p>
<p>“Who sent you?”</p>
<p>“Captain Gérard.”</p>
<p>Raymond pulled free.</p>
<p>“He commands my household guard.”</p>
<p>That was enough for Raymond.</p>
<p>It was not enough for Alaric.</p>
<p>But before he could stop him, shouting erupted from the gatehouse.</p>
<p>The courtyard surged with soldiers.</p>
<p>For less than a minute, Alaric lost sight of Raymond.</p>
<p>When he saw the messenger again, the man was running.</p>
<p>Alone.</p>
<p>Alaric’s stomach dropped.</p>
<p>He pushed through the crowd toward the eastern passage.</p>
<p>Raymond was gone.</p>
<h1>Chapter 8 – The Rescue Mission</h1>
<p>They found Raymond’s sword in the stable yard.</p>
<p>Not broken.</p>
<p>Dropped.</p>
<p>Beside it lay one of his household guards with blood on his temple and no memory of anything after opening the side gate.</p>
<p>Charles stood over the sword in silence.</p>
<p>No one needed to say what had happened.</p>
<p>Bernier had escaped the trap inside Bellacourt and succeeded in the more important part of his plan.</p>
<p>He had taken the heir-presumptive.</p>
<p>“Close every road,” Charles said.</p>
<p>“Already done, Your Majesty,” replied the captain of the guard.</p>
<p>“Then search every wagon, chapel, manor and farm within ten leagues.”</p>
<p>Alaric looked at the ground.</p>
<p>Four horses had left the stable yard at speed.</p>
<p>One carried more weight than the others.</p>
<p>The tracks turned east.</p>
<p>“They are not hiding him near the city,” Alaric said.</p>
<p>Charles looked at him.</p>
<p>“How do you know?”</p>
<p>“Because Bernier spoke about taking Raymond outside Bellacourt before dawn. And because if he wants leverage, he needs somewhere defensible.”</p>
<p>Gaston arrived carrying a bundle of hastily gathered maps.</p>
<p>“Bernier has estates north and east of the capital.”</p>
<p>“He will not take Raymond to his own castle,” Charles said. “Too obvious.”</p>
<p>Alaric studied the map.</p>
<p>“Then somewhere he thinks you cannot reach quickly.”</p>
<p>A royal scout pointed to a road running toward the wooded hills east of Bellacourt.</p>
<p>“There are old toll forts here. Most are abandoned. A few belong to minor lords.”</p>
<p>“And beyond them?” Alaric asked.</p>
<p>“The marches.”</p>
<p>Charles’s jaw tightened.</p>
<p>“And roads toward men who do not answer to me.”</p>
<p>Lothar.</p>
<p>No one said the name, but Alaric thought it.</p>
<p>The Dragon Riders were ready within minutes.</p>
<p>Without their dragons, they looked almost ordinary.</p>
<p>Almost.</p>
<p>They wore Bretonnian riding gear over their own armor where it fit, carried swords and short spears, and rode the fastest horses Charles could provide.</p>
<p>Master Bertrand watched them mount in the dark.</p>
<p>“You have spent a week learning not to disgrace yourselves on horseback,” he said.</p>
<p>“Encouraging,” Alaric replied.</p>
<p>“Now try not to die proving how much you learned.”</p>
<p>The gates opened.</p>
<p>They rode east.</p>
<p>The pursuit was unlike flying.</p>
<p>On Vaelor, Alaric could cross forests, rivers and walls in a straight line. From the sky, roads were suggestions.</p>
<p>On horseback, every stream mattered.</p>
<p>Every muddy slope mattered.</p>
<p>Every bridge could decide whether they gained or lost an hour.</p>
<p>For the first time since arriving in Bretonnia, Alaric understood why so much power was built around roads, tolls, bridges and fortified crossings.</p>
<p>They followed the kidnappers through the eastern farms and into rougher country.</p>
<p>At dawn they found the first abandoned horse.</p>
<p>Foam covered its neck. One shoe was missing.</p>
<p>“They are pushing too hard,” Gaston said.</p>
<p>“Good,” Alaric answered. “So are we.”</p>
<p>By midday they reached a fork where the tracks split.</p>
<p>Two horses continued east.</p>
<p>Two turned north.</p>
<p>A rider swore.</p>
<p>“Decoy.”</p>
<p>Alaric dismounted.</p>
<p>He walked both tracks.</p>
<p>The eastern pair were clean and fast.</p>
<p>The northern tracks were deeper.</p>
<p>One horse had stumbled repeatedly.</p>
<p>“Raymond went north,” he said.</p>
<p>“How can you tell?”</p>
<p>“The extra weight.”</p>
<p>He pointed to the earth.</p>
<p>“And whoever is riding behind is not sitting willingly.”</p>
<p>The riders turned north.</p>
<p>The land rose into wooded ridges.</p>
<p>Near sunset they saw smoke above the trees.</p>
<p>Gaston checked the map.</p>
<p>“There is an old watch fort ahead. It guarded the road before Charles’s grandfather moved the border east.”</p>
<p>“Who owns it?”</p>
<p>“Officially? A lord who has probably not visited it in twenty years.”</p>
<p>“Perfect place to hide the heir to a kingdom.”</p>
<p>They left the horses in a hollow and approached on foot.</p>
<p>The fort was little more than a stone tower joined to a low wall. One gate faced the road. A second, narrower postern opened toward the forest.</p>
<p>Alaric counted six guards outside.</p>
<p>“Bernier?” whispered one Rider.</p>
<p>“Not visible.”</p>
<p>“Raymond?”</p>
<p>“Inside.”</p>
<p>“How do you know?”</p>
<p>A shout came from the tower.</p>
<p>“Because that sounds like him.”</p>
<p>Even through stone, Raymond’s voice carried remarkably well.</p>
<p>“If you intend to ransom me, at least have the courtesy to explain your price!”</p>
<p>A guard shouted back for him to be quiet.</p>
<p>Raymond answered with something impolite.</p>
<p>Alaric smiled.</p>
<p>“He is fine.”</p>
<p>The rescue plan was simple because complicated plans had too many opportunities to fail.</p>
<p>Two riders circled through the woods toward the postern.</p>
<p>Gaston and another rider remained with the horses.</p>
<p>Alaric approached the main gate openly.</p>
<p>The guards saw him halfway down the road.</p>
<p>“Stop!”</p>
<p>He stopped.</p>
<p>“I am Alaric of the Dragon Rider Order.”</p>
<p>That produced exactly the reaction he wanted.</p>
<p>They knew the name.</p>
<p>They also knew there was no dragon behind him.</p>
<p>The captain stepped onto the wall.</p>
<p>“Turn around.”</p>
<p>“You have Lord Raymond.”</p>
<p>“We have no one.”</p>
<p>From inside the tower came Raymond’s voice.</p>
<p>“ALERIC!”</p>
<p>The captain closed his eyes briefly.</p>
<p>Alaric looked up at him.</p>
<p>“Very convincing.”</p>
<p>The men on the wall shifted nervously.</p>
<p>They were not soldiers fighting for a cause.</p>
<p>They were retainers who had been told to hold a tower for money or loyalty and had now discovered that the most famous knight in the Valley was standing outside asking questions.</p>
<p>Alaric rested one hand on the pommel of his sword.</p>
<p>“Open the gate.”</p>
<p>“You are alone.”</p>
<p>“No.”</p>
<p>The captain looked toward the trees.</p>
<p>That was the mistake.</p>
<p>Behind the fort, the postern burst inward.</p>
<p>The two Dragon Riders struck from inside.</p>
<p>Alaric ran for the gate as shouting erupted above him.</p>
<p>One guard lowered a spear.</p>
<p>Alaric knocked it aside, seized the shaft and pulled the man off balance. Another swung a sword. Alaric blocked, drove his shoulder into him and reached the gatehouse before the captain could organize a defense.</p>
<p>The fight lasted less than two minutes.</p>
<p>Most of the guards surrendered as soon as they realized men were already inside the wall.</p>
<p>Alaric climbed the tower stairs two at a time.</p>
<p>He found Raymond behind a barred door.</p>
<p>Raymond had somehow acquired a wooden stool and was holding it like a weapon.</p>
<p>“You took your time.”</p>
<p>“I was admiring the architecture.”</p>
<p>Alaric broke the bar loose.</p>
<p>Raymond stepped out.</p>
<p>“Bernier left hours ago.”</p>
<p>Alaric’s expression changed.</p>
<p>“Where?”</p>
<p>“East. He met riders here during the night. Not Bretonnian, I think. Different shields. Different accents.”</p>
<p>“Lothar?”</p>
<p>“I heard the name once.”</p>
<p>That was worse than Alaric had hoped.</p>
<p>Outside, one of the captured guards confirmed it.</p>
<p>Bernier had delivered messages and ridden east with several foreign-looking horsemen. Raymond had been left behind under guard.</p>
<p>“Why leave him?” Gaston asked.</p>
<p>Raymond looked back toward the tower.</p>
<p>“Because they expected the prison to hold me while they moved on to the next part.”</p>
<p>Alaric nodded.</p>
<p>“Taking you was not a decoy. It threatened the succession and gave Bernier leverage over Charles.”</p>
<p>“And while he was trying to remove me,” Raymond said, “Lothar’s army was already moving.”</p>
<p>The rescued heir mounted one of the spare horses.</p>
<p>For once, he was not joking.</p>
<p>They began the return to Bellacourt before sunset.</p>
<p>Halfway back, they passed a column of royal soldiers moving east.</p>
<p>The captain saluted Raymond and immediately turned his men around to escort him home.</p>
<p>Alaric rode beside Gaston.</p>
<p>“The kidnapping failed,” he said. “But the military plan did not depend on its success.”</p>
<p>Gaston nodded.</p>
<p>“Bernier and Lothar prepared both at the same time.”</p>
<p>Behind them, Raymond was explaining to a royal officer exactly how badly the tower guards had tied his hands.</p>
<p>Ahead, Bellacourt’s towers were still invisible beyond the hills.</p>
<p>Alaric thought of Vaelor in the Valley.</p>
<p>For the first time since the dragons had left, he wished the distance between them were much shorter.</p>
<h1>Chapter 9 – Attack!</h1>
<p>They saw the smoke before they saw Bellacourt.</p>
<p>At first Alaric thought it came from the city itself.</p>
<p>A dark line lay across the eastern horizon, too broad for a single fire and too low for a storm cloud.</p>
<p>Raymond rose in his saddle.</p>
<p>“That is near the river road.”</p>
<p>Gaston shaded his eyes.</p>
<p>“No,” he said. “Closer.”</p>
<p>The riders pushed their horses harder.</p>
<p>By midday they began to meet people fleeing west.</p>
<p>Farm carts. Shepherds. Merchants with half-loaded wagons. A priest riding beside three children on a mule.</p>
<p>Alaric stopped the priest.</p>
<p>“What happened?”</p>
<p>“Soldiers.”</p>
<p>“Whose?”</p>
<p>The priest shook his head.</p>
<p>“Not the king’s. They came before dawn. Took the east bridge, burned the watch house, cut the road.”</p>
<p>Raymond looked at Alaric.</p>
<p>“Lothar.”</p>
<p>The name no longer sounded distant.</p>
<p>A second group of refugees arrived from the north road.</p>
<p>Their story was worse.</p>
<p>Two warning beacons had never been lit.</p>
<p>One local captain had ordered his men to remain in barracks because a sealed royal message claimed the movements were only tournament traffic.</p>
<p>Another patrol had been sent twenty miles in the wrong direction.</p>
<p>None of it was enough by itself to open a kingdom.</p>
<p>Together, it created hours.</p>
<p>And hours were what an army needed.</p>
<p>Raymond swore.</p>
<p>“Bernier.”</p>
<p>Gaston nodded grimly.</p>
<p>“He did not improvise this after the feast.”</p>
<p>“No,” Alaric said. “He was already moving pieces.”</p>
<p>The riders reached the first royal outpost before sunset.</p>
<p>It had been abandoned in haste.</p>
<p>A broken signal horn lay beside the gate.</p>
<p>Inside, they found two wounded soldiers and a lieutenant trying to organize a dozen men.</p>
<p>“How close?” Raymond asked.</p>
<p>The lieutenant stared at him in disbelief.</p>
<p>“My lord? We were told you had been taken.”</p>
<p>“I was.”</p>
<p>The man seemed unsure what to do with that answer.</p>
<p>Raymond pointed east.</p>
<p>“How close is Lothar?”</p>
<p>“His vanguard crossed the river this morning. Main force behind. Infantry, cavalry, wagons. More men than we have here by twenty times.”</p>
<p>“Bellacourt warned?”</p>
<p>“We sent a rider.”</p>
<p>“Did he arrive?”</p>
<p>The lieutenant hesitated.</p>
<p>“We do not know.”</p>
<p>Alaric looked at the roads.</p>
<p>Bellacourt lay behind them to the west.</p>
<p>The enemy was moving from the east and northeast.</p>
<p>If they returned immediately, they might enter the city before the roads closed.</p>
<p>If they remained to help the outpost, they risked being cut off outside.</p>
<p>Raymond made the choice first.</p>
<p>“We go home.”</p>
<p>Alaric nodded.</p>
<p>“Agreed.”</p>
<p>They rode through the night.</p>
<p>Bellacourt appeared at dawn.</p>
<p>Its walls were already crowded.</p>
<p>Banners snapped above the towers. Carts jammed the road to the gates. Soldiers shouted at peasants and merchants trying to enter at once.</p>
<p>Beyond the eastern fields, cavalry moved like dark insects along the ridges.</p>
<p>Lothar’s scouts.</p>
<p>The city gates remained open just long enough.</p>
<p>Raymond crossed first.</p>
<p>Alaric followed with the Dragon Riders.</p>
<p>The gate captain recognized them and shouted for the portcullis to remain raised.</p>
<p>A minute later, the last wagon squeezed through.</p>
<p>Then the chains groaned.</p>
<p>The gate came down.</p>
<p>Outside, a horn answered from the hills.</p>
<p>Bellacourt was not yet surrounded.</p>
<p>But it soon would be.</p>
<p>Charles was waiting in Côte de Lys.</p>
<p>He had not slept.</p>
<p>Maps covered the same tables where, only days earlier, Anselm had organized the response to the sickness.</p>
<p>The similarities were impossible to miss.</p>
<p>Except now the marks on the maps were soldiers.</p>
<p>Raymond entered first.</p>
<p>Charles crossed the room and embraced him.</p>
<p>For a moment the king said nothing.</p>
<p>Then he stepped back.</p>
<p>“You were told to remain inside the citadel.”</p>
<p>Raymond looked at Alaric.</p>
<p>“Can we postpone that conversation until after the invasion?”</p>
<p>“No.”</p>
<p>Charles stared at him.</p>
<p>Then he exhaled.</p>
<p>“Yes.”</p>
<p>Alaric almost smiled.</p>
<p>Almost.</p>
<p>“What do we know?” he asked.</p>
<p>Charles pointed to the map.</p>
<p>“Too much and not enough.”</p>
<p>Lothar’s army had crossed into the kingdom along three roads.</p>
<p>The fastest warning route had been sabotaged.</p>
<p>Two small forts had received forged orders and yielded their bridges without a fight.</p>
<p>Several lesser nobles loyal to Bernier had delayed mobilizing.</p>
<p>Others had obeyed Charles immediately but could not concentrate their men in time.</p>
<p>The tournament itself had made things worse.<